This pairs a default method on an interface with a stateful abstract class, so the split is visible side by side:

```java
public class DefaultVsAbstract {
    // Interface + default method: shared behavior, no state, multiple inheritance
    interface Greeter {
        String name();
        default String greet() { return "Hi, " + name(); } // default body
    }

    // Abstract class: shared behavior + instance state + constructor
    abstract static class Account {
        protected double balance;                 // instance state
        Account(double opening) { balance = opening; }
        void deposit(double a) { balance += a; }
        abstract String type();                   // subclass contract
    }

    record Person(String name) implements Greeter {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new Person("Sam").greet()); // Hi, Sam
    }
}
```

Greeter shares behavior with no state, so a record can implement it freely. Account needs a balance field and a constructor to seed it, which only an abstract class can provide.

Here several Java 8 features cooperate in one short pipeline:

```java
public class OrderProcessor {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepository;

    public Optional<Order> findHighestValueOrder(Long customerId) {
        return orderRepository.findByCustomerId(customerId) // returns List<Order>
            .stream()                                        // convert to Stream
            .filter(order -> order.getStatus() == COMPLETED) // lambda filtering
            .max(Comparator.comparing(Order::getTotalValue)) // method reference
            .map(this::addLoyaltyBonus);                     // method reference to instance method
    }

    private Order addLoyaltyBonus(Order order) {
        // returns new Order with bonus applied - immutable transformation
        return new Order(order.getId(), 
                        order.getCustomerId(),
                        order.getTotalValue() * 1.1);
    }

    public void processOrder(Long orderId) {
        findOrderById(orderId)                           // returns Optional<Order>
            .filter(order -> order.getTotalValue() > 100)// Optional.filter uses lambda
            .ifPresent(this::sendConfirmationEmail);     // method reference, executed only if present
    }

    private void sendConfirmationEmail(Order order) {
        // send email logic
    }
}
```

One chain moves through a stream, a lambda filter, two method references, and an Optional result, which shows how tightly these features interlock.
